Let me address each of the four risks the way I would when hardening a nightly job.

MEMORY first. The naive `repository.findAll()` loads every row into a `List` and every entity into the persistence context simultaneously. For millions of rows this exhausts the heap. There are two robust fixes. One is PAGING with a cursor (keyset pagination): fetch a bounded page, process it, then fetch the next page using the last id seen. The other is a scrollable `Stream` with a small JDBC fetch size. Either way, after each chunk I call `entityManager.flush()` then `entityManager.clear()` so the processed entities detach and become eligible for garbage collection; without the clear, the persistence context keeps growing and defeats the purpose.

```java
String cursor = "";
Page<Account> page;
do {
    page = accountRepo.findNextChunk(cursor, PageRequest.of(0, CHUNK));   // keyset, bounded
    for (Account a : page.getContent()) { recalcInterest(a); }
    entityManager.flush();
    entityManager.clear();                                                // release memory
    if (!page.isEmpty()) cursor = page.getContent().getLast().getId();
} while (!page.isEmpty());
```

LONG EXECUTION second. A single run over millions of rows can last hours and might overlap the next trigger. I bound the work: either process the whole set in chunks within one run but commit per chunk so progress is durable, or cap each run to a time/row budget and resume next time from the checkpoint. I also prevent overlap — by default Spring's single-threaded scheduler will not start the next run until the current finishes, but if I use a pool I guard with a lock so two runs never process the same data.

DB LOAD third. Unbounded queries, huge `IN` clauses, and tight loops can saturate the database and starve live banking traffic. I use bounded chunk reads, a sensible `fetchSize`, batched writes (`jdbc.batch_size`), and I throttle — a small pause between chunks or a rate limiter — so the job is a good citizen. Where the data is read-mostly I run against a read replica, and I schedule the heavy job off-peak.

ERRORS last, and most important for correctness. If the job runs as one giant transaction and fails at row nine million, everything rolls back and the night is wasted. Instead I use PER-CHUNK transactions: each chunk commits independently, so completed chunks stay done. I record a CHECKPOINT (the last processed cursor) so a restart resumes where it stopped — this makes the job RESUMABLE and IDEMPOTENT (re-running a chunk must be safe, so I write updates that are safe to repeat). For individual bad records (a "poison" row that always fails), I SKIP-AND-LOG rather than aborting the chunk, record the failure for later review, and continue. I add metrics and alerts so operations knows how far the job got and whether it failed.

```java
@Transactional                                   // PER-CHUNK transaction
public void processChunk(List<Account> chunk) {
    for (Account a : chunk) {
        try { recalcInterest(a); }
        catch (Exception e) { log.warn("skip account {}: {}", a.getId(), e.getMessage()); /* record poison */ }
    }
}
```

The senior recommendation: for genuinely large, restartable, auditable batch work, use SPRING BATCH, which gives chunk-oriented processing, a `JobRepository` that persists progress, restartability, skip/retry policies, and metrics out of the box. A hand-written paged loop is fine for moderate volumes, but Spring Batch is the production-grade answer when the dataset is large and the job must be resumable and observable. Also, the `@Scheduled` method itself should delegate to a service quickly and not hold the scheduler thread doing heavy work inline if other scheduled tasks must run.

